    "['isis enable [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0 level 2', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0 level 2\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0 level 2\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The HUAWEI command `isis enable 1` enables IS-IS on an interface with process ID 1. \\n- In Juniper, we explicitly configure IS-IS under the protocols hierarchy and specify interfaces and their levels.\\n- Since the HUAWEI config shows IS-IS level-2 (is-level level-2), I translated this to Juniper's level 2 configuration for both the loopback and physical interfaces.\\n- Note: Juniper uses different interface naming (ge-1/0/0.0 for GigabitEthernet1/0/0 and lo0.0 for Loopback1).\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0 level 2'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0 level 2', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0 level 2\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0 level 2\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The HUAWEI command `isis enable 1` enables IS-IS on an interface with process ID 1. \\n- In Juniper, we explicitly configure IS-IS under the protocols hierarchy and specify interfaces and their levels.\\n- Since the HUAWEI config shows IS-IS level-2 (is-level level-2), I translated this to Juniper's level 2 configuration for both the loopback and physical interfaces.\\n- Note: Juniper uses different interface naming (ge-1/0/0.0 for GigabitEthernet1/0/0 and lo0.0 for Loopback1).\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0 level 2'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'unit [parameter2] family [parameter3] [parameter4] [parameter5]', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls rsvp-te hello', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols rsvp interface all hello-interval 9', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Juniper equivalent command for `mpls rsvp-te hello`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols rsvp interface all hello-interval 9\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses a global hello interval (default 9 seconds) rather than per-interface configuration like Huawei.\\n2. The \"all\" keyword applies to all RSVP-enabled interfaces. You could also specify individual interfaces if needed.\\n3. The hello interval value (9 seconds) is the typical default in Juniper, matching Huawei\\'s default behavior when enabling RSVP-TE hello.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols rsvp interface all hello-interval 9'}]]": 1,
    "['isis enable [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0\\n##  \\n\\nThe `isis enable 1` command in Huawei activates IS-IS on an interface with process ID 1. In Juniper, the equivalent is achieved by specifying the interfaces under IS-IS protocol configuration.  \\n\\nNote:  \\n1. Juniper uses interface naming like `ge-1/0/0.0` (with unit/encapsulation)  \\n2. Loopback is typically `lo0.0` in Juniper  \\n3. Unlike Huawei's process-based model, Juniper applies IS-IS globally to interfaces without explicit process IDs.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0\\n##  \\n\\nThe `isis enable 1` command in Huawei activates IS-IS on an interface with process ID 1. In Juniper, the equivalent is achieved by specifying the interfaces under IS-IS protocol configuration.  \\n\\nNote:  \\n1. Juniper uses interface naming like `ge-1/0/0.0` (with unit/encapsulation)  \\n2. Loopback is typically `lo0.0` in Juniper  \\n3. Unlike Huawei's process-based model, Juniper applies IS-IS globally to interfaces without explicit process IDs.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0'}]]": 1,
    "['ip address unnumbered interface [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces lo0 unit 0 family inet address 1.1.1.1/32', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces lo0 unit 0 family inet address 1.1.1.1/32\\nset interfaces gr-0/0/0 unit 0 tunnel source 1.1.1.1\\nset interfaces gr-0/0/0 unit 0 family inet unnumbered-address lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Juniper, the equivalent of \"ip address unnumbered interface LoopBack1\" is achieved using the `unnumbered-address` directive pointing to the loopback interface (lo0.0).\\n2. I used `gr-0/0/0` as a placeholder for the tunnel interface name (Tunnel10 in Huawei). Replace it with the actual Juniper tunnel interface name (e.g., `ge-0/0/0` or a GRE interface if applicable).\\n3. The loopback address must first be explicitly configured in Juniper.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces lo0 unit 0 family inet address 1.1.1.1/32'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces gr-0/0/0 unit 0 tunnel source 1.1.1.1', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces lo0 unit 0 family inet address 1.1.1.1/32\\nset interfaces gr-0/0/0 unit 0 tunnel source 1.1.1.1\\nset interfaces gr-0/0/0 unit 0 family inet unnumbered-address lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Juniper, the equivalent of \"ip address unnumbered interface LoopBack1\" is achieved using the `unnumbered-address` directive pointing to the loopback interface (lo0.0).\\n2. I used `gr-0/0/0` as a placeholder for the tunnel interface name (Tunnel10 in Huawei). Replace it with the actual Juniper tunnel interface name (e.g., `ge-0/0/0` or a GRE interface if applicable).\\n3. The loopback address must first be explicitly configured in Juniper.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces gr-0/0/0 unit 0 tunnel source 1.1.1.1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces gr-0/0/0 unit 0 family inet unnumbered-address lo0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces lo0 unit 0 family inet address 1.1.1.1/32\\nset interfaces gr-0/0/0 unit 0 tunnel source 1.1.1.1\\nset interfaces gr-0/0/0 unit 0 family inet unnumbered-address lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Juniper, the equivalent of \"ip address unnumbered interface LoopBack1\" is achieved using the `unnumbered-address` directive pointing to the loopback interface (lo0.0).\\n2. I used `gr-0/0/0` as a placeholder for the tunnel interface name (Tunnel10 in Huawei). Replace it with the actual Juniper tunnel interface name (e.g., `ge-0/0/0` or a GRE interface if applicable).\\n3. The loopback address must first be explicitly configured in Juniper.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces gr-0/0/0 unit 0 family inet unnumbered-address lo0.0'}]]": 1,
    "['tunnel-protocol [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls traffic-engineering tunnels', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols mpls traffic-engineering tunnels\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if you specifically need the tunnel interface configuration equivalent (which is more precise for the given command), it would be:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces tunnel unit 10 tunnel protocol mpls-traffic-engineering\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper equivalent depends on context. The first translation enables MPLS TE globally, while the second reflects the specific tunnel interface configuration. Since the original command was under a tunnel interface, the second translation is more accurate.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls traffic-engineering tunnels'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces tunnel unit 10 tunnel protocol mpls-traffic-engineering', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols mpls traffic-engineering tunnels\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if you specifically need the tunnel interface configuration equivalent (which is more precise for the given command), it would be:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces tunnel unit 10 tunnel protocol mpls-traffic-engineering\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper equivalent depends on context. The first translation enables MPLS TE globally, while the second reflects the specific tunnel interface configuration. Since the original command was under a tunnel interface, the second translation is more accurate.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces tunnel unit 10 tunnel protocol mpls-traffic-engineering'}]]": 1,
    "['destination [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'to 5.5.5.5;', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##  \\nto 5.5.5.5;  \\n##  \\n\\nExplanation:  \\n- In Juniper's MPLS TE tunnel configuration under `interface` > `gr-X/X/X` (or logical tunnel), the `destination` parameter is translated to `to` followed by the endpoint IP address.  \\n- The semicolon (`;`) is added as Juniper uses it as a statement terminator.  \\n- The original Huawei command specifies the tunnel's destination (egress LSR ID), which is preserved in the Juniper syntax.\", 'trans_template': 'to 5.5.5.5;'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls te record-route label', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel10 record', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel10 record\\n##', 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel10 record'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls te fast-reroute', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls interface all fast-reroute', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols mpls interface all fast-reroute\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if the context requires a more specific tunnel-focused fast-reroute configuration (common in Juniper for RSVP-TE tunnels), it could also be expressed as:\\n##\\nset protocols rsvp interface all fast-reroute\\n##\\n\\nNote: The exact Juniper equivalent depends on the deployment context. The first option is MPLS-wide, while the second focuses on RSVP-TE (which Huawei's `mpls te fast-reroute` implies). Both are valid translations.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls interface all fast-reroute'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols rsvp interface all fast-reroute', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols mpls interface all fast-reroute\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if the context requires a more specific tunnel-focused fast-reroute configuration (common in Juniper for RSVP-TE tunnels), it could also be expressed as:\\n##\\nset protocols rsvp interface all fast-reroute\\n##\\n\\nNote: The exact Juniper equivalent depends on the deployment context. The first option is MPLS-wide, while the second focuses on RSVP-TE (which Huawei's `mpls te fast-reroute` implies). Both are valid translations.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols rsvp interface all fast-reroute'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls te tunnel-id [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path PE1-to-PE2 tunnel-id 100', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper equivalent command for `mpls te tunnel-id 100`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path PE1-to-PE2 tunnel-id 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the tunnel-id is configured under the label-switched-path (LSP) configuration context rather than directly under the interface. The LSP name (PE1-to-PE2 in this example) would typically reflect the source-to-destination naming convention.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path PE1-to-PE2 tunnel-id 100'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls te path explicit-path [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path tope2 to 5.5.5.5', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper equivalent command for `mpls te path explicit-path tope2`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path tope2 to 5.5.5.5\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path tope2 primary explicit-path tope2\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Juniper, the explicit path would need to be separately configured under `set protocols mpls path tope2` with the same hop sequence as in the Huawei configuration.\\n2. The destination address (5.5.5.5) is taken from the tunnel configuration's destination parameter in the original Huawei config.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path tope2 to 5.5.5.5'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path tope2 primary explicit-path tope2', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper equivalent command for `mpls te path explicit-path tope2`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path tope2 to 5.5.5.5\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path tope2 primary explicit-path tope2\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Juniper, the explicit path would need to be separately configured under `set protocols mpls path tope2` with the same hop sequence as in the Huawei configuration.\\n2. The destination address (5.5.5.5) is taken from the tunnel configuration's destination parameter in the original Huawei config.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path tope2 primary explicit-path tope2'}]]": 1
}